A||N||2||Cplensdorf||Help!  3.1L or 2.8L?||||1||
Z||000000||Cplensdorf||03-26-2001||02:32 PM||||OK....I am looking for a little advice.  I've got two engines.  The first is a brand new (no rebuild) 88 GT crate motor(2.8) it is 95% complete from flywheel to upper intake. The second is a rebuilt bored and 'stroked' ('87)2.8 shortblock, I think it is around a 3.2L (?) I am looking at placing the upper end of the 'new' 88 engine (intakes, injectors, etc.) on the '87 block.  Are there any issues I should be concerned with.  How do I know if the engine is internally or exernally balanced?  Is there a difference between the 87 and 88 engines?  <BR>Also, I anticipate a turbo upgrade after the install.  Any problems with this?<P>Thanks in advance.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||03-26-2001||08:11 PM||||If it has a 3.1 crankshaft than it’s neutrally balanced or it should be. The crankshaft will have a cast in ring in the middle with notches in it used for a crankshaft sensor. You will need a neutrally balanced flywheel for either engine. The 88 flywheel is a neutrally balanced flywheel. <P>See my thread on the differences in compression ratios between the 2.8 and stroked 3.1 (originally a 2.8) motors. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html</A>  <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-26-2001||08:18 PM||||I don't know what the crate 2.8 has for a bottom end but if you are going to turbo you may want to stick with that. High compression with boost is a bad thing. ||||reg||1
